opponent raised to 300 from small blind)\n- Stack: 19,900 chips\n- Opponent's action: Raised to 300 (a 3x big blind raise)\n- Cost to call: 200 more chips (I already put in 100 as big blind)\n\n**Hand Strength:**\nK-6 off-suit is a weak hand in heads-up play. It's considered a marginal hand at best:\n- It's dominated by any hand with a King and better kicker (KQ, KJ, K10, K9, K8, K7)\n- It's roughly 50-50 against random hands, but loses to premium pairs and better Kings\n- It has some high card value with the King\n\n**Strategic Considerations:**\n1. **Fold (-1)**: Costs nothing more, avoids playing a weak hand out of position\n2. **Call (0)**: Risk 200 to see the flop with pot odds of 400:200 (2:1). Not terrible odds, but K6o is weak\n3. **Raise (600+)**: Would be a re-raise representing strength, but K6o is too weak to build a big pot with\n\n**Game Context:**\n- This is hand 17/20, near the end of the game\n- I'm ahead by 2,150 chips cumulatively\n- I have a slight chip lead this hand (19,900 vs opponent's implied ~20,100)\n- Being conservative might be wise to protect my lead\n\n**Decision:**\nWith K-6 off-suit facing a 3x raise, this is typically a fold in standard play. However, in heads-up poker, ranges are wider. The 2:1 pot odds make calling marginally acceptable, but the hand plays poorly post-flop and I'll be out of position.\n\nReason: K-6 off-suit is too weak to justify building a pot against a raise, especially out of position. While heads-up play demands wider ranges, this hand is dominated by too many likely holdings and plays poorly post-flop. With a comfortable chip lead in the overall match, there's no need to gamble with marginal hands. Folding preserves chips for better spots.\n\nAnswer: -1"}
